{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000611","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 candidates are direct matches for urinary bladder cancer/bladder cancer (trait_reported and phenotyping_reported align to bladder cancer; one uses the near-synonymous urothelial carcinoma endpoint). No candidate reports clearly PRS-only AUROC/R2; most AUROC values shown are full-model and not directly comparable across differing covariate sets, and some very high full-model AUROCs (e.g., ~0.80 in PGS000071/PGS000782) are likely covariate-driven per the provided domain guidance.

Among the closely comparable Cancer PRSweb bladder cancer models, PGS000611 (GWAS Hits, 13 variants) stands out with the strongest visible effect-size evidence in this set via an OR(top 1% vs rest)=2.66 (1.79–3.93) and a moderate per-SD OR=1.284 (1.211–1.361), with endpoint “Cancer of bladder”, basic covariates (age/sex/batch/PCs), European development and evaluation, and a reasonably sized validation sample (n=13,530). Competing PRSweb siblings (PGS000610/PGS000612/PGS000613) have similar per-SD ORs and AUROCs but weaker (or less clearly superior) extreme-tail ORs, while genome-wide shrinkage models (e.g., PGS000607/PGS000608/PGS004687) show low full-model AUROC (~0.52–0.56) and do not provide stronger PRS-comparable metrics here.

Main limitation: because PRS-only discrimination metrics are absent across candidates and many reported AUROCs are full-model and covariate-dependent, the choice cannot be elevated to high confidence; several near-equivalent direct-match alternatives remain plausible given the incomplete comparability of performance reporting."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000613","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 listed candidates are direct matches to the target (bladder cancer / urinary bladder carcinoma/cancer) with largely EUR GWAS/EVAL context. Most candidates do not report PRS-only AUC/R²; many reported AUROCs are full-model and therefore not directly comparable for PRS ranking (per domain guidance), especially when covariates differ.

Within the largest coherent endpoint-consistent cluster (Cancer PRSweb bladder cancer models), the UKB-derived GWAS-hits/P+T models show materially stronger effect size evidence (OR per SD ~1.28–1.30) and better full-model AUROC (~0.567–0.572) than the PRS-CS/lassosum variants (AUROC ~0.519–0.538; OR per SD ~1.08–1.14). Between the two strongest siblings, PGS000613 (P+T; 15 variants; DEV UKB; EVAL n=13,530) slightly edges PGS000611 (GWAS hits; 13 variants; similar endpoint/covariates) by having the strongest OR per SD (1.301 vs 1.284) and a slightly higher top-1% vs rest OR (2.91 vs 2.66), with nearly identical AUROC and matched covariate set.

Main limitation: no candidate provides PRS-only AUROC/R² for bladder cancer in this context, so discrimination comparisons rely on effect sizes and non-PRS-only metrics; additionally, very high full-model AUROC values in Graff et al / Fritsche-style models (PGS000071/PGS000782) are likely covariate-driven (age/smoking/BMI) and cannot be treated as PRS strength evidence, preventing a high-confidence pick."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS003381","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates for the target concept (uterine cancer) are present, including broad uterine/uterus cancer labels (PGS000541, PGS000542, PGS001795, PGS003740) and the clinically dominant subtype endometrial/uterine endometrial carcinoma (PGS000075, PGS000786, PGS002300, PGS002735–PGS002737, PGS004244, PGS003381). Per domain guidance, endometrial carcinoma is an acceptable strong direct match for uterine cancer (dominant subtype), so selection should prioritize endpoint fidelity plus PRS-comparable evidence.

PGS003381 is preferred because it targets the dominant uterine cancer subtype with explicit endpoint phrasing (phenotyping_reported: uterine endometrial carcinoma), uses a genome-wide method (lassosum; 529,365 variants—structurally more polygenic than GWAS-hits scores like PGS000541/PGS000542/PGS003740), and reports comparatively strong discrimination in the selected European validation (full-model AUROC 0.761; R² 0.11) with relatively basic covariates (age, top 20 PCs). In contrast, the exact-label uterine cancer framework score PGS001795 comes from a global biobank framework context and shows weaker PRS-comparable evidence (PRS-only R² 0.001948; AUROC 0.66), and the uterus-cancer GWAS-hits scores (PGS000541/PGS000542) have lower AUROC (~0.57) despite clean covariates.

Main limitation: the AUROC/R² reported for PGS003381 appear to be full-model metrics (not explicitly PRS-only), making cross-model comparability imperfect; therefore confidence is Moderate rather than High."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002032",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All three candidates are direct matches to the target concept (bilirubin metabolism disease) via trait_efo, with PGS001824 and PGS002032 having the most endpoint-specific phenotype label (“Disorders of bilirubin excretion”). Between those two near-clones (same publication, training cohort UKB, covariates, evaluation ancestries, and identical partial-r=0.0716), PGS002032 is structurally better supported because it uses a genome-wide LDpred2 approach with many more variants (19,768 vs 22), which is a positive structural signal when other evidence is effectively identical. PGS000924 reports high AUROC/full-model metrics, but it comes from a pan-trait sparse UKB framework (“across 813 traits”), has very low variant count (5), and uses a time-to-event composite phenotype (“Disorders of porphyrin and bilirubin metabolism”), which together make its apparent performance less reliable for selecting the best standalone PRS. Overall, the evidence is still limited (no PGS-only AUROC for the preferred endpoint-specific models; partial-r is modest and not directly comparable to AUROC/R²), so the direct-match recommendation is sub-optimal rather than high-confidence."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002032",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All three candidates are direct matches to the target concept (bilirubin metabolism disease) via trait_efo, but they differ in endpoint fidelity and evidence quality. PGS001824 and PGS002032 have the closest phenotype alignment (phenotyping_reported/trait_reported: “Disorders of bilirubin excretion”) and come from the same portability framework paper with identical reported partial correlation (partial-r=0.0716) and similar covariates, making them the strongest endpoint-aligned cluster in this context. Between those two near-clones, PGS002032 is structurally preferred because it is an LDpred2 genome-wide model with far more variants (19,768 vs 22), which is a supportive structural signal when other evidence is indistinguishable. PGS000924 reports strong-looking AUROC values, but it is from a pan-trait sparse UKB framework (“across 813 traits”), uses a time-to-event composite endpoint (“Disorders of porphyrin and bilirubin metabolism”), and its very high full-model AUROC is not clearly PRS-only; domain guidance flags such framework sparse models and time-to-event endpoints as warning signals and advises not to over-rank based on full-model AUROC. Overall, the choice remains sub-optimal because the best endpoint-aligned models (PGS001824/PGS002032) lack PRS-comparable AUROC/R2 and only provide partial-r, limiting confidence in comparative performance."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000327",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Both candidates are direct matches by trait_reported/trait_efo, but PGS000327 has clearly better endpoint fidelity: phenotyping_reported is explicitly “Autism spectrum disorder” (aligned with the target), whereas PGS002790’s phenotyping_reported is “Psychiatric behavior (withdrawn)”, which is an explicit mismatch/endpoint ambiguity despite the ASD label fields. PGS000327 also provides more supportive PRS-quality evidence (OR per SD=1.33 and R²=0.0245 in European validation) than PGS002790 (partial R²=0.00546; no effect sizes shown). Main limitation: neither model reports a clearly PRS-only (no-covariates) AUROC/R², and PGS000327’s reported R² appears to be a full-model metric, so PRS-only performance remains uncertain, lowering confidence from high."
}
